Chapter: GopFat16
  cites Works chapter GopDisk
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16
  cites Foreword chapter Fat32
  cites Foreword chapter CCE
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 FAT over the payload's own block drivers (B3.2, FAT32 in H3b). The
 foreword `Fat16` chapter already knows FAT: its BPB parse,
 directory-entry decode, name matching, and path splitting are pure
 functions over a sector buffer, and this chapter reuses every one of
 them. What it cannot reuse is `Fat16`'s sector source: those
 functions call the `block-read-sector` builtin, which is a syscall.
 The Option A stub jumps straight to `opening` and configures no
 syscall path, so on this boot every read must go through
 `disk-read-sector` (AHCI, then legacy IDE).

 The same mount also speaks FAT32 -- the format every vendor stick
 and every volume past two gigabytes actually carries. The volume
 record is shared: a FAT32 volume is recognized structurally (a zero
 16-bit FAT size names the 32-bit layout, the same key Linux turns),
 its root-entry count is zero by construction (which is exactly how
 the rest of this chapter tells the two apart), vol-root-start holds
 the root directory's CLUSTER rather than a sector, and vol-fat-sectors
 comes from the 32-bit field. Three rules follow the width everywhere:
 FAT entries are four bytes whose top nibble is reserved (masked on
 read, PRESERVED on write); end-of-chain is a 28-bit compare, never
 the 16-bit sentinel -- on a large volume a perfectly valid cluster
 number exceeds 65528 and truncating it to the FAT16 test silently
 ends every long chain; and a directory entry's starting cluster
 carries a high word at offset 20 that the 16-bit decoder never reads.

 Reading a file writes bytes into a flat caller-supplied buffer.
 `Fat16`'s own reader accumulates a `List Integer` one byte at a time --
 fine for a small config file, ruinous for a 2 MB seed, which is what
 B3.3 will ask for.

 We say:

Section: GPT

 The EFI System Partition's first LBA, found by walking the GPT: the
 header at LBA 1 names the partition-entry array, and the first entry
 whose type GUID is the ESP's carries the starting LBA. Returns 0 when
 the disk has no GPT or no ESP -- an honest zero, not a guess.

  esp-type-guid-b0 : Integer = 40
  esp-type-guid-b1 : Integer = 115
  esp-type-guid-b2 : Integer = 42
  esp-type-guid-b3 : Integer = 193

  gpt-sig-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  gpt-sig-ok (buf) =
    peek-byte buf 0 == 69 & peek-byte buf 1 == 70 & peek-byte buf 2 == 73
    & peek-byte buf 3 == 32 & peek-byte buf 4 == 80 & peek-byte buf 5 == 65
    & peek-byte buf 6 == 82 & peek-byte buf 7 == 84

 Only the low 32 bits of each 64-bit GPT field are read: a boot medium
 with more than 2 TB of sectors before its ESP is not a stick.

  gpt-esp-start : [Device.Port] Integer
  gpt-esp-start = act
    hdr <- disk-read-sector 1
    if hdr.dr-ok == False then 0
    else if gpt-sig-ok (hdr.dr-buf) == False then 0
    else act
      ents <- disk-read-sector (fat16-read-u32 (hdr.dr-buf) 72)
      if ents.dr-ok then gpt-scan-entries (ents.dr-buf) 0 else 0
    end
  end

 Four bytes of the type GUID are enough to name the ESP among the
 partition types a boot stick carries.

  gpt-is-esp : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gpt-is-esp (buf) (off) =
    peek-byte buf off == esp-type-guid-b0
    & peek-byte buf (off + 1) == esp-type-guid-b1
    & peek-byte buf (off + 2) == esp-type-guid-b2
    & peek-byte buf (off + 3) == esp-type-guid-b3

  gpt-scan-entries : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gpt-scan-entries (buf) (i) =
    if i >= 4 then 0
    else let off = i * 128
    in if gpt-is-esp buf off then fat16-read-u32 buf (off + 32)
    else gpt-scan-entries buf (i + 1)

Section: Mount

  GfatMount = record {
    gm-ok : Boolean,
    gm-vol : Fat16Volume
  }

  gfat-null-vol : Fat16Volume
  gfat-null-vol = Fat16Volume {
    vol-part-start = 0, vol-bytes-per-sector = 0, vol-sectors-per-cluster = 0,
    vol-reserved-sectors = 0, vol-num-fats = 0, vol-root-entry-count = 0,
    vol-fat-sectors = 0, vol-fat-start = 0, vol-root-start = 0, vol-data-start = 0,
    vol-total-sectors = 0, vol-cluster-count = 0
  }

 A zero 16-bit FAT size is the structural mark of the 32-bit layout;
 the parse then maps FAT32's fields into the shared record as the
 chapter prose describes.

  gfat-is32 : Fat16Volume -> Boolean
  gfat-is32 (vol) = vol.vol-root-entry-count == 0

  gfat-parse-bpb : Integer, Integer -> Fat16Volume
  gfat-parse-bpb (part-start) (buf) =
    if fat16-read-u16 buf 22 /= 0 then fat16-parse-bpb part-start buf
    else let bps = fat16-read-u16 buf 11
    in let spc = peek-byte buf 13
    in let reserved = fat16-read-u16 buf 14
    in let nfats = peek-byte buf 16
    in let fatsz = fat16-read-u32 buf 36
    in let fat-start = part-start + reserved
    in let data-start = fat-start + nfats * fatsz
    in let total = fat16-total-sectors buf
    in let data-sectors = total - (data-start - part-start)
    in let clusters = if spc == 0 then 0 else if data-sectors <= 0 then 0 else data-sectors / spc
    in Fat16Volume {
      vol-part-start = part-start,
      vol-bytes-per-sector = bps,
      vol-sectors-per-cluster = spc,
      vol-reserved-sectors = reserved,
      vol-num-fats = nfats,
      vol-root-entry-count = 0,
      vol-fat-sectors = fatsz,
      vol-fat-start = fat-start,
      vol-root-start = fat16-read-u32 buf 44,
      vol-data-start = data-start,
      vol-total-sectors = total,
      vol-cluster-count = clusters
    }

  gfat-mount-at : Integer -> [Device.Port] GfatMount
  gfat-mount-at (part-start) = act
    b <- disk-read-sector part-start
    if b.dr-ok == False then GfatMount { gm-ok = False, gm-vol = gfat-null-vol }
    else let vol = gfat-parse-bpb part-start (b.dr-buf)
    in if vol.vol-bytes-per-sector /= 512 then GfatMount { gm-ok = False, gm-vol = gfat-null-vol }
    else GfatMount { gm-ok = True, gm-vol = vol }
  end

  gfat-mount-esp : [Device.Port] GfatMount
  gfat-mount-esp = act
    start <- gpt-esp-start
    if start == 0 then GfatMount { gm-ok = False, gm-vol = gfat-null-vol }
    else gfat-mount-at start
  end

Section: FAT Chain

 End-of-chain follows the volume's width: the 16-bit sentinel test on
 a FAT32 volume would silently end every chain at the first cluster
 number past 65528, which a large volume reaches in ordinary use. A
 failed FAT read answers end-of-chain in the right width, so a bad
 sector truncates a file instead of spinning the walk.

  gfat-is-end : Fat16Volume, Integer -> Boolean
  gfat-is-end (vol) (cluster) =
    if gfat-is32 vol then fat32-is-end cluster else fat16-is-end cluster

  gfat-next-cluster : Fat16Volume, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-next-cluster (vol) (cluster) = act
    if gfat-is32 vol then act
      b <- disk-read-sector ((vol.vol-fat-start) + (cluster * 4) / (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector))
      if b.dr-ok then bit-and (fat16-read-u32 (b.dr-buf) (int-mod (cluster * 4) (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector))) 268435455 else 268435455
    end
    else act
      b <- disk-read-sector ((vol.vol-fat-start) + (cluster * 2) / (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector))
      if b.dr-ok then fat16-read-u16 (b.dr-buf) (int-mod (cluster * 2) (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)) else 65535
    end
  end

Section: Directory Search

 On FAT32 a directory entry's starting cluster has a high word at
 offset 20 that the 16-bit decoder never reads; a file placed past
 cluster 65535 on a big volume would otherwise resolve to the wrong
 low-word cluster and read someone else's bytes. The 32-bit scanner
 mirrors the foreword's walk exactly -- free entry ends the
 directory, long-name and label entries are skipped -- and fixes the
 decoded entry's cluster.

  gfat-read-entry32 : Integer, Integer -> Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-read-entry32 (buf) (off) =
    let e = fat16-read-dir-entry buf off
    in __record-set e "de-cluster" (bit-or (e.de-cluster) (bit-shl (fat16-read-u16 buf (off + 20)) 16))

  gfat-scan-entries32 : Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Maybe Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-scan-entries32 (buf) (name) (i) (count) =
    if i >= count then None
    else let off = i * 32
    in if fat16-is-free-entry buf off then
      if peek-byte buf off == 0 then None
      else gfat-scan-entries32 buf name (i + 1) count
    else if fat16-is-lfn-entry buf off then gfat-scan-entries32 buf name (i + 1) count
    else if fat16-is-volume-label buf off then gfat-scan-entries32 buf name (i + 1) count
    else let entry = gfat-read-entry32 buf off
    in if fat16-name-matches (entry.de-name) name then Just entry
    else gfat-scan-entries32 buf name (i + 1) count

  gfat-scan-entries : Fat16Volume, Integer, Text -> Maybe Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-scan-entries (vol) (buf) (name) =
    if gfat-is32 vol then gfat-scan-entries32 buf name 0 ((vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) / 32)
    else fat16-scan-sector-entries buf name 0 ((vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) / 32)

 The FAT32 root directory is an ordinary cluster chain named by the
 BPB, so its search IS the data-area search; only FAT16 has the fixed
 root region.

  gfat-find-in-root : Fat16Volume, Text -> [Device.Port] Maybe Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-find-in-root (vol) (name) =
    if gfat-is32 vol then gfat-find-in-dir vol (vol.vol-root-start) name
    else let root-sectors = ((vol.vol-root-entry-count) * 32 + (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) - 1) / (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)
    in gfat-scan-root vol name (vol.vol-root-start) root-sectors

  gfat-scan-root : Fat16Volume, Text, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Maybe Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-scan-root (vol) (name) (sector) (remaining) = act
    if remaining <= 0 then None
    else act
      b <- disk-read-sector sector
      if b.dr-ok == False then None
      else when gfat-scan-entries vol (b.dr-buf) name
        is Just (entry) -> Just entry
        is None -> gfat-scan-root vol name (sector + 1) (remaining - 1)
    end
  end

 A directory that lives in the data area is a cluster chain, so a miss
 in one cluster follows the FAT to the next.

  gfat-find-in-dir : Fat16Volume, Integer, Text -> [Device.Port] Maybe Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-find-in-dir (vol) (cluster) (name) = act
    if gfat-is-end vol cluster then None
    else if cluster < 2 then None
    else act
      hit <- gfat-scan-cluster vol name (fat16-cluster-sector vol cluster) (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster)
      when hit
        is Just (entry) -> Just entry
        is None -> act
          nxt <- gfat-next-cluster vol cluster
          gfat-find-in-dir vol nxt name
        end
    end
  end

  gfat-scan-cluster : Fat16Volume, Text, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Maybe Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-scan-cluster (vol) (name) (sector) (remaining) = act
    if remaining <= 0 then None
    else act
      b <- disk-read-sector sector
      if b.dr-ok == False then None
      else when gfat-scan-entries vol (b.dr-buf) name
        is Just (entry) -> Just entry
        is None -> gfat-scan-cluster vol name (sector + 1) (remaining - 1)
    end
  end

Section: Path Resolution

  gfat-resolve : Fat16Volume, Text -> [Device.Port] Maybe Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-resolve (vol) (path) = act
    let parts = fat16-split-path path
    in if list-length parts == 0 then None
    else act
      first <- gfat-find-in-root vol (list-at parts 0)
      when first
        is None -> None
        is Just (e) -> gfat-walk vol parts 1 (list-length parts) e
    end
  end

 `e` is the entry matched at depth-1. A path continues only through a
 directory (attribute bit 4); a file in the middle of a path is a miss,
 not a silent success.

  gfat-walk : Fat16Volume, List Text, Integer, Integer, Fat16DirEntry -> [Device.Port] Maybe Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-walk (vol) (parts) (depth) (total) (e) = act
    if depth >= total then Just e
    else if bit-and (e.de-attr) 16 /= 16 then None
    else act
      nxt <- gfat-find-in-dir vol (e.de-cluster) (list-at parts depth)
      when nxt
        is None -> None
        is Just (e2) -> gfat-walk vol parts (depth + 1) total e2
    end
  end

Section: Directory Listing

 The file manager wants every entry, not a name match: the same
 sector walk as the search, collecting decoded entries instead of
 comparing them. Per the spec a free entry whose first byte is zero
 ends the directory globally, not per sector -- the collector stops
 within its sector and the walk re-scans the same in-memory buffer
 for the terminal byte before fetching another sector, so stale
 garbage past the terminal on a dirty volume is never presented as
 files. Dot entries are dropped: the caller navigates upward itself,
 and "." is noise in every view. One walk serves both widths: the
 fixed FAT16 root passes a negative cluster and simply runs out of
 sectors; a chained directory follows the FAT when its cluster's
 sectors are exhausted.

  gfat-dot-entry : Text -> Boolean
  gfat-dot-entry (name) = if name == "." then True else name == ".."

  gfat-collect-entries : Fat16Volume, Integer, List Fat16DirEntry -> List Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-collect-entries (vol) (buf) (acc) =
    gfat-collect-loop vol buf 0 ((vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) / 32) acc

  gfat-collect-loop : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Fat16DirEntry -> List Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-collect-loop (vol) (buf) (i) (count) (acc) =
    if i >= count then acc
    else let off = i * 32
    in if fat16-is-free-entry buf off then
      if peek-byte buf off == 0 then acc
      else gfat-collect-loop vol buf (i + 1) count acc
    else if fat16-is-lfn-entry buf off then gfat-collect-loop vol buf (i + 1) count acc
    else if fat16-is-volume-label buf off then gfat-collect-loop vol buf (i + 1) count acc
    else let entry = if gfat-is32 vol then gfat-read-entry32 buf off else fat16-read-dir-entry buf off
    in if gfat-dot-entry (entry.de-name) then gfat-collect-loop vol buf (i + 1) count acc
    else gfat-collect-loop vol buf (i + 1) count (list-push acc entry)

  gfat-sector-has-end : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gfat-sector-has-end (buf) (count) = gfat-has-end-loop buf 0 count

  gfat-has-end-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gfat-has-end-loop (buf) (i) (count) =
    if i >= count then False
    else if peek-byte buf (i * 32) == 0 then True
    else gfat-has-end-loop buf (i + 1) count

  gfat-list-walk : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Fat16DirEntry -> [Device.Port] List Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-list-walk (vol) (sector) (remaining) (cluster) (acc) = act
    if remaining <= 0 then
      if cluster < 0 then acc
      else act
        nxt <- gfat-next-cluster vol cluster
        if gfat-is-end vol nxt then acc
        else if nxt < 2 then acc
        else gfat-list-walk vol (fat16-cluster-sector vol nxt) (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster) nxt acc
      end
    else act
      b <- disk-read-sector sector
      if b.dr-ok == False then acc
      else let acc2 = gfat-collect-entries vol (b.dr-buf) acc
      in if gfat-sector-has-end (b.dr-buf) ((vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) / 32) then acc2
      else gfat-list-walk vol (sector + 1) (remaining - 1) cluster acc2
    end
  end

  gfat-list-dir : Fat16Volume, Integer -> [Device.Port] List Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-list-dir (vol) (cluster) =
    if cluster < 2 then []
    else gfat-list-walk vol (fat16-cluster-sector vol cluster) (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster) cluster []

  gfat-list-root : Fat16Volume -> [Device.Port] List Fat16DirEntry
  gfat-list-root (vol) =
    if gfat-is32 vol then gfat-list-dir vol (vol.vol-root-start)
    else let root-sectors = ((vol.vol-root-entry-count) * 32 + (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) - 1) / (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)
    in gfat-list-walk vol (vol.vol-root-start) root-sectors (-1) []

Section: File Reading

 Copy a file's bytes into `dest`, following the FAT chain. Returns the
 number of bytes written, which is the entry's size unless the chain
 ended early (a truncated or corrupt file -- report what was actually
 read, never what the directory claimed).

  gfat-read-into : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-read-into (vol) (cluster) (size) (dest) =
    gfat-read-loop vol cluster size dest 0 0

  gfat-read-loop : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-read-loop (vol) (cluster) (remaining) (dest) (written) (sector-off) = act
    if remaining <= 0 then written
    else if gfat-is-end vol cluster then written
    else if cluster < 2 then written
    else act
      b <- disk-read-sector (fat16-cluster-sector vol cluster + sector-off)
      if b.dr-ok == False then written
      else let bps = vol.vol-bytes-per-sector
      in let n = if remaining < bps then remaining else bps
      in let c = gfat-copy (b.dr-buf) dest written n 0
      in let off2 = sector-off + 1
      in if off2 >= (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster)
        then act
          nxt <- gfat-next-cluster vol cluster
          gfat-read-loop vol nxt (remaining - n) dest (written + n) 0
        end
        else gfat-read-loop vol cluster (remaining - n) dest (written + n) off2
    end
  end

  gfat-copy : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfat-copy (src) (dest) (dest-off) (n) (i) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let w = poke-byte dest (dest-off + i) (peek-byte src i)
    in gfat-copy src dest dest-off n (i + 1)

  GfatFile = record {
    gf-ok : Boolean,
    gf-buf : Integer,
    gf-len : Integer
  }

  gfat-read-file : Fat16Volume, Text -> [Device.Port] GfatFile
  gfat-read-file (vol) (path) = act
    e <- gfat-resolve vol path
    when e
      is None -> GfatFile { gf-ok = False, gf-buf = 0, gf-len = 0 }
      is Just (entry) -> act
        let dest = __heap-save
        in let adv = __heap-advance (entry.de-size + 8)
        in act
          n <- gfat-read-into vol (entry.de-cluster) (entry.de-size) dest
          GfatFile { gf-ok = n == entry.de-size, gf-buf = dest, gf-len = n }
        end
      end
  end

 Disk bytes are ASCII; `code-to-char` wants a CCE code, so the byte goes
 through `from-unicode` first.

Section: Bulk File Reading

 Reading a 2 MB file one sector at a time costs two reads per sector: the
 data, and the FAT sector holding the next cluster. Instead load the FAT
 once, follow the chain in memory, and coalesce consecutive clusters into
 runs -- a freshly written file is contiguous, so a whole seed becomes a
 single bulk command (one DMA on AHCI). Fragmentation costs one command
 per run, never per sector.

  GfatFat = record {
    gft-ok : Boolean,
    gft-buf : Integer
  }

  gfat-load-fat : Fat16Volume -> [Device.Port] GfatFat
  gfat-load-fat (vol) = act
    let n = vol.vol-fat-sectors
    in let buf = __heap-save
    in let adv = __heap-advance (n * 512 + 8)
    in act
      ok <- disk-read-into (vol.vol-fat-start) n buf
      GfatFat { gft-ok = ok, gft-buf = buf }
    end
  end

  gfat-fat-next : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfat-fat-next (vol) (fat) (cluster) =
    if gfat-is32 vol then bit-and (fat16-read-u32 fat (cluster * 4)) 268435455
    else fat16-read-u16 fat (cluster * 2)

 Length of the run of consecutive clusters starting at `cluster`, capped
 so a corrupt chain cannot spin: a run can never exceed the clusters the
 file still needs.

  gfat-run-length : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfat-run-length (vol) (fat) (cluster) (cap) (n) =
    if n >= cap then n
    else let nxt = gfat-fat-next vol fat cluster
    in if nxt == cluster + 1 then gfat-run-length vol fat (cluster + 1) cap (n + 1)
    else n + 1

  gfat-read-runs : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-read-runs (vol) (fat) (cluster) (sectors-left) (dest) (written) = act
    if sectors-left <= 0 then written
    else if gfat-is-end vol cluster then written
    else if cluster < 2 then written
    else act
      let spc = vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster
      in let clusters-left = (sectors-left + spc - 1) / spc
      in let run = gfat-run-length vol fat cluster clusters-left 0
      in let run-sectors = if run * spc > sectors-left then sectors-left else run * spc
      in act
        ok <- disk-read-into (fat16-cluster-sector vol cluster) run-sectors (dest + written * 512)
        if ok == False then written
        else act
          nxt <- gfat-fat-next vol fat (cluster + run - 1)
          gfat-read-runs vol fat nxt (sectors-left - run-sectors) dest (written + run-sectors)
        end
      end
    end
  end

 Read a file into a caller-supplied buffer. The buffer must hold the
 file's size rounded up to a whole sector -- the last sector arrives
 whole, and trimming it is the caller's business.

  gfat-read-file-bulk : Fat16Volume, Text, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-read-file-bulk (vol) (path) (dest) = act
    e <- gfat-resolve vol path
    when e
      is None -> 0
      is Just (entry) -> act
        f <- gfat-load-fat vol
        if f.gft-ok == False then 0
        else act
          let sectors = (entry.de-size + 511) / 512
          in act
            got <- gfat-read-runs vol (f.gft-buf) (entry.de-cluster) sectors dest 0
            if got * 512 >= entry.de-size then entry.de-size else 0
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end

  gfat-file-size : Fat16Volume, Text -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-file-size (vol) (path) = act
    e <- gfat-resolve vol path
    when e
      is None -> 0
      is Just (entry) -> entry.de-size
    end
  end

  gfat-text : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  gfat-text (buf) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else gfat-text buf (i + 1) n (acc & char-to-text (code-to-char (from-unicode (peek-byte buf i))))

Section: Writing

 A single-cluster file writer, enough to persist an identity (a wrapped
 key and its salt/iv/pubkey fit in one 512-byte cluster). It finds a free
 cluster, writes the data there, marks the cluster end-of-chain in both
 FAT copies, and adds or replaces a root directory entry. Larger files
 need a cluster chain; that is a later increment, guarded by the size
 check below.

 The 8.3 name is passed already space-padded to 11 characters, the way it
 sits in a directory entry ("IDENTITYDAT").

  gfat-put-u16 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfat-put-u16 (buf) (off) (v) =
    let a = poke-byte buf off (bit-and v 255)
    in poke-byte buf (off + 1) (bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255)

  gfat-put-u32 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfat-put-u32 (buf) (off) (v) =
    let a = gfat-put-u16 buf off (bit-and v 65535)
    in gfat-put-u16 buf (off + 2) (bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 65535)

 Scan the loaded FAT for the first free entry at cluster 2 or above.
 Free means the entry's CLUSTER bits are zero: FAT32's top nibble is
 reserved and may be dirty on a volume other tools have touched, so
 the read masks it off. Returns 0 when the volume is full.

  gfat-find-free : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfat-find-free (vol) (fat) (cluster) (max-cluster) =
    if cluster >= max-cluster then 0
    else if gfat-fat-next vol fat cluster == 0 then cluster
    else gfat-find-free vol fat (cluster + 1) max-cluster

 Set one FAT entry in BOTH copies. Each copy is edited by reading the
 sector holding the entry, poking the bytes, and writing it back --
 a FAT with two disagreeing copies is a corrupt volume. On FAT32 the
 entry's reserved top nibble is preserved, as the spec demands.

  gfat-set-fat-entry : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  gfat-set-fat-entry (vol) (cluster) (value) (copy) = act
    let width = if gfat-is32 vol then 4 else 2
    in let byte-off = cluster * width
    in let sec-in-fat = byte-off / (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)
    in let off-in-sec = int-mod byte-off (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)
    in let lba = (vol.vol-fat-start) + copy * (vol.vol-fat-sectors) + sec-in-fat
    in act
      b <- disk-read-sector lba
      if b.dr-ok == False then False
      else let p = if gfat-is32 vol
        then gfat-put-u32 (b.dr-buf) off-in-sec (bit-or (bit-and (fat16-read-u32 (b.dr-buf) off-in-sec) 4026531840) (bit-and value 268435455))
        else gfat-put-u16 (b.dr-buf) off-in-sec value
      in disk-write-into lba 1 (b.dr-buf)
    end
  end

  gfat-eoc : Fat16Volume -> Integer
  gfat-eoc (vol) = if gfat-is32 vol then 268435455 else 65535

  gfat-mark-eoc : Fat16Volume, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  gfat-mark-eoc (vol) (cluster) = act
    a <- gfat-set-fat-entry vol cluster (gfat-eoc vol) 0
    if a == False then False
    else if vol.vol-num-fats < 2 then True
    else gfat-set-fat-entry vol cluster (gfat-eoc vol) 1
  end

 Find a root directory slot for `name`: reuse an existing entry with the
 same name (so a re-save overwrites rather than duplicates), else the
 first free slot. Returns the (sector, offset) as sector*4096+offset, or
 -1 if the root directory is full.

  gfat-root-slot : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-root-slot (vol) (name-buf) (sector) (remaining) (checked) = act
    if remaining <= 0 then -1
    else if checked >= (vol.vol-root-entry-count) then -1
    else act
      b <- disk-read-sector sector
      if b.dr-ok == False then -1
      else let hit = gfat-slot-in-sector (b.dr-buf) name-buf 0 ((vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) / 32)
      in if hit >= 0 then sector * 4096 + hit
      else gfat-root-slot vol name-buf (sector + 1) (remaining - 1) (checked + ((vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) / 32))
    end
  end

 The FAT32 root has no fixed region; the slot search walks its cluster
 chain and answers in the same sector*4096+offset encoding. A full
 last cluster answers -1 rather than allocating an extension -- the
 identity re-save that motivates this writer reuses its own slot.

  gfat-dir-slot32 : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-dir-slot32 (vol) (name-buf) (cluster) = act
    if gfat-is-end vol cluster then -1
    else if cluster < 2 then -1
    else act
      hit <- gfat-slot-in-cluster vol name-buf (fat16-cluster-sector vol cluster) (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster)
      if hit >= 0 then hit
      else act
        nxt <- gfat-next-cluster vol cluster
        gfat-dir-slot32 vol name-buf nxt
      end
    end
  end

  gfat-slot-in-cluster : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-slot-in-cluster (vol) (name-buf) (sector) (remaining) = act
    if remaining <= 0 then -1
    else act
      b <- disk-read-sector sector
      if b.dr-ok == False then -1
      else let hit = gfat-slot-in-sector (b.dr-buf) name-buf 0 ((vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) / 32)
      in if hit >= 0 then sector * 4096 + hit
      else gfat-slot-in-cluster vol name-buf (sector + 1) (remaining - 1)
    end
  end

  gfat-slot-in-sector : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfat-slot-in-sector (buf) (name-buf) (i) (count) =
    if i >= count then -1
    else let off = i * 32
    in if gfat-name-eq buf off name-buf then off
    else let first = peek-byte buf off
    in if first == 0 | first == 229 then off
    else gfat-slot-in-sector buf name-buf (i + 1) count

  gfat-name-eq : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gfat-name-eq (buf) (off) (name-buf) = gfat-name-eq-loop buf off name-buf 0

  gfat-name-eq-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  gfat-name-eq-loop (buf) (off) (name-buf) (i) =
    if i >= 11 then True
    else if peek-byte buf (off + i) /= peek-byte name-buf i then False
    else gfat-name-eq-loop buf off name-buf (i + 1)

 Write a directory entry into the located root slot: the 11-byte name,
 attribute 0x20 (archive), the starting cluster, and the size. The rest
 of the 32-byte entry is zeroed. The cluster's high word always lands
 at offset 20 -- zero on FAT16, load-bearing on FAT32.

  gfat-write-dir-entry : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  gfat-write-dir-entry (name-buf) (slot) (cluster) (size) (bytes-per-sector) = act
    let lba = slot / 4096
    in let off = int-mod slot 4096
    in act
      b <- disk-read-sector lba
      if b.dr-ok == False then False
      else let buf = b.dr-buf
      in let nm = gfat-copy name-buf buf off 11 0
      in let z = gfat-zero-range buf (off + 11) 21
      in let at = poke-byte buf (off + 11) 32
      in let ch = gfat-put-u16 buf (off + 20) (bit-shru cluster 16)
      in let cl = gfat-put-u16 buf (off + 26) (bit-and cluster 65535)
      in let sz = gfat-put-u32 buf (off + 28) size
      in disk-write-into lba 1 buf
    end
  end

  gfat-zero-range : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gfat-zero-range (buf) (off) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf off 0
    in gfat-zero-range buf (off + 1) (n - 1)

 Write `size` bytes from `src` as a one-cluster file named `name-buf`
 (11 bytes, space-padded). Returns True only if the free cluster, both
 FAT copies, the cluster data, and the directory entry all wrote. The
 FSInfo sector's free-count hint is left untouched -- it is advisory
 by spec, and a stale hint costs a later allocator a scan, not
 correctness.

  gfat-write-small-file : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  gfat-write-small-file (vol) (name-buf) (src) (size) = act
    if size > (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) * (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster) then False
    else act
      f <- gfat-load-fat vol
      if f.gft-ok == False then False
      else act
        let width = if gfat-is32 vol then 4 else 2
        in let max-cluster = (vol.vol-fat-sectors) * (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) / width
        in let free = gfat-find-free vol (f.gft-buf) 2 max-cluster
        in if free == 0 then False
        else act
          eoc <- gfat-mark-eoc vol free
          if eoc == False then False
          else act
            dw <- disk-write-into (fat16-cluster-sector vol free) (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster) src
            if dw == False then False
            else act
              slot <- gfat-find-slot vol name-buf
              if slot < 0 then False
              else gfat-write-dir-entry name-buf slot free size (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end

  gfat-find-slot : Fat16Volume, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gfat-find-slot (vol) (name-buf) = act
    if gfat-is32 vol then gfat-dir-slot32 vol name-buf (vol.vol-root-start)
    else gfat-root-slot vol name-buf (vol.vol-root-start) (((vol.vol-root-entry-count) * 32 + (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) - 1) / (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)) 0
  end
